  [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m — [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m.

  [1mThe consequence:[0m this is a session lifetime, so it's a security change, not just a number. Going from 30 minutes to 8
  hours means a stolen or hijacked session token stays valid for 16x longer, and an unattended logged-in browser (shared
  machine, kiosk, laptop left open) stays authenticated for a full workday instead of expiring over a lunch break. The
  size of the edit is one line; the blast radius isn't.

  One related thing worth knowing: [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m is the only consumer here, and it just logs the value — there's no
  idle-vs-absolute distinction in this code. So 8 hours would be an absolute session lifetime with no inactivity cutoff,
  which is the more permissive of the two readings.

  Confirm and I'll make the change exactly as asked — just the value, nothing else.

